    "result_text": "Tests are green. Here's how I handled each finding:\n\n## Finding 1 \u2014 remove `govern: [ transaction ]` from the audit step \u2014 **Agreed, fixed**\n\nThe audit step neither reads nor writes the database; it only logs. Per AGENTS.md, `transaction` governance is for writes and `readonly-transaction` for reads, so carrying either on a pure-logging step is off-spec. Removing it also better matches \"on successful create\" by letting the write governance close before the audit line is emitted. I dropped the `govern` line from the `audit` step:\n\n```yaml\naudit:\n  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.AuditOwnerCreated\n  next: respond\n```\n\n## Finding 2 \u2014 rename `service` \u2192 `audit` \u2014 **Declined (finding is incorrect for this codebase)**\n\nThe finding claims the existing steps use descriptive verb method names. That's true of the *illustrative* worked example in AGENTS.md, but not of the actual code: all 25 function classes in the owner package \u2014 including `SaveOwner`, `RespondWithOwnerCreated`, `AssignCustomerCode`, `BuildOwner`, `ResolveRegistrationDate` \u2014 define their single public method as `service`. The verb lives in the *class* name (`AuditOwnerCreated`), and the YAML step key (`audit`) supplies the step-level verb. Renaming this one method to `audit` would make it the lone outlier among its 25 siblings, reducing consistency rather than improving it. The \"exactly one public method\" rule is satisfied either way, so I kept `service`.\n\nThe single acceptance test (`coreLogsAuditOnCreate`, the only test in the suite) passes: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` / `BUILD SUCCESS`, and the `AUDIT` line still carries the id, customerCode, and registrationDate.",
