    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new `AuditOwnerCreated` class is the right call \u2014 a dedicated, single-responsibility step placed after `save` so the generated id is available is exactly the idiomatic shape this codebase uses (one small function class per step). Two structural issues, though:\n\n1. **`govern: [ transaction ]` does not belong on the audit step.** Per AGENTS.md, `transaction` governance is for *writes* (`readonly-transaction` for reads); an audit step neither reads nor writes the database, it only logs. Carrying the governance here extends the transactional span to wrap a pure logging call, which is both unnecessary and semantically backwards for an audit of a *successful* create: with governance on `audit`, the log line is emitted *before* the commit, so a create that later fails to commit would still have been logged as created. Dropping `govern` from the `audit` step lets `save`'s governance close (commit) first, and the audit line is written only after the successful commit \u2014 which matches the spec's \"on successful create.\" So:\n\n   ```yaml\n   audit:\n     class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.AuditOwnerCreated\n     next: respond\n   ```\n\n2. **Method name `service` is off-convention.** The worked examples and existing steps in this project use a descriptive verb method name matching the step's job (`validate`, `load`, `apply`, `save`, `respond`), not a generic `service`. Rename to `audit` (or similar) so it reads like its siblings:\n\n   ```java\n   public void audit(@Val Owner owner) { ... }\n   ```\n\nBoth are localized to the new file plus the one YAML step; the overall placement and decomposition are otherwise clean.",
